WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.920
What if I told you that the longer an artificial intelligence thinks about your question,

00:06.380 --> 00:09.540
the more it actively forgets what you just asked it?

00:09.660 --> 00:11.940
I mean, it sounds completely counterintuitive, right?

00:11.940 --> 00:18.520
Right. And what if I told you that this active, forgetting this, well, this seemingly bizarre glitch in the machine

00:18.520 --> 00:21.820
might actually be the mathematical secret to intelligence itself?

00:21.980 --> 00:26.580
Yeah, not just in silicon either, but potentially in your own biological brain.

00:26.580 --> 00:33.900
Exactly. Because we're so conditioned to believe that thinking harder means gathering more details,

00:34.440 --> 00:35.880
you know, holding on to more information.

00:36.020 --> 00:39.540
Yeah, we definitely are. But the data is telling a completely different story.

00:39.700 --> 00:43.460
It really is. And, you know, usually when we talk about trying to understand something complex,

00:43.520 --> 00:49.500
like a medical diagnosis or a piece of software, there's this baseline expectation of precision.

00:49.680 --> 00:50.860
Like a static snapshot.

00:51.000 --> 00:53.320
Yeah, like an x-ray. You break your arm, you go to the hospital,

00:53.320 --> 00:56.380
and the x-ray shows that jagged white line on the bone,

00:56.520 --> 00:59.420
and the doctor just points at it and says, there is the problem.

00:59.580 --> 01:03.860
Right. It's a very comforting way to view the world. We crave that binary clarity.

01:04.200 --> 01:05.200
Broken or not broken.

01:05.420 --> 01:05.680
Yeah.

01:05.900 --> 01:06.460
True or false.

01:06.980 --> 01:10.640
Exactly. We want to be able to take a static snapshot of a system,

01:11.540 --> 01:16.320
freeze it in time, point to the components, and understand exactly what we are looking at.

01:16.320 --> 01:22.400
But then you step into the world of artificial intelligence, specifically how these massive neural networks,

01:22.540 --> 01:26.440
you know, the large language models we interact with daily, actually think.

01:26.620 --> 01:31.080
And suddenly that x-ray machine we rely on to understand the world is entirely useless.

01:31.260 --> 01:32.120
Completely useless.

01:32.120 --> 01:37.060
We are looking at a diagnostic landscape that is incredibly murky.

01:37.400 --> 01:42.700
It's like, imagine trying to figure out the complex, twisting plot of a two-hour mystery movie,

01:43.240 --> 01:48.320
but you are only allowed to look at one single blurry photograph taken randomly at the 45-minute mark.

01:48.580 --> 01:52.360
That captures the frustration of modern AI interpretability perfectly.

01:52.920 --> 01:53.040
Yeah.

01:53.040 --> 01:59.440
You're trying to infer a dynamic, evolving, moving process from a completely static point in time.

01:59.440 --> 02:03.500
Yeah. And for a long time, the entire field of AI research has been banging its head against the wall,

02:03.940 --> 02:05.760
trying to make sense of those static snapshots.

02:05.800 --> 02:08.200
Assuming the snapshots themselves contained all the answers.

02:08.500 --> 02:10.660
Which is exactly why we are here today.

02:10.880 --> 02:15.440
We are going on a deep dive into a fascinating, frankly, mind-bending mystery,

02:15.800 --> 02:18.540
straight from the bleeding edge of AI research.

02:18.700 --> 02:19.800
And it is a dense one.

02:20.020 --> 02:20.500
Oh, for sure.

02:20.740 --> 02:25.360
We're digging into a June 2026 paper published by Flyxion Independent Research.

02:25.880 --> 02:27.680
The title is A Bit of a Mouthful.

02:27.680 --> 02:29.700
It's called States Without Histories.

02:30.460 --> 02:33.960
Latent Reasoning as Admissibility Field Traversal.

02:34.300 --> 02:36.500
It is an intensely academic title, yes.

02:36.700 --> 02:37.520
Very academic.

02:37.840 --> 02:40.920
But the implications buried under that jargon are staggering.

02:41.580 --> 02:43.920
This paper is causing a massive stir right now.

02:44.120 --> 02:48.520
So the mission for our deep dives today is to decode this paradigm-shifting paper.

02:48.800 --> 02:51.440
Because what the researchers at Flyxion are arguing is that

02:51.440 --> 02:56.780
our foundational definition of what a thought actually is might be entirely fundamentally wrong.

02:56.780 --> 02:59.360
Both in artificial neural networks and biologically.

02:59.640 --> 02:59.720
Right.

02:59.840 --> 03:02.400
And we've brought together this rigorous theoretical paper,

03:02.560 --> 03:04.900
along with the broader context of the field,

03:05.020 --> 03:11.000
because this paper is actually a direct pointed response to some earlier, highly publicized research.

03:11.240 --> 03:11.500
Yes.

03:11.620 --> 03:13.840
To really grasp the magnitude of the Flyxion paper,

03:13.900 --> 03:16.140
we have to start with the research they're responding to.

03:16.500 --> 03:17.620
The Sedeck and Fard paper.

03:17.620 --> 03:18.420
Exactly.

03:18.740 --> 03:21.980
The work of Sedeck and Fard published earlier in 2026.

03:22.380 --> 03:27.920
They really set the stage for this entire current debate in the AI community.

03:28.120 --> 03:34.080
They introduced this core tension that has literally been keeping AI researchers up at night.

03:34.480 --> 03:37.520
And the tension is this phenomenon I mentioned at the top of the show.

03:38.220 --> 03:43.900
Large language models seem to forget the specific problem they are solving the longer they compute it.

03:43.900 --> 03:47.880
Yet somehow, miraculously, they still spit out the right answer at the end.

03:48.080 --> 03:49.220
Okay, let's unpack this.

03:49.280 --> 03:51.620
I want to spend some real time here so we understand the baseline.

03:52.100 --> 03:54.780
Walk us through this initial discovery by Sedeck and Fard.

03:54.880 --> 03:56.980
What exactly were they doing and what were they seeing?

03:57.620 --> 04:00.420
Well, to understand the mystery Sedeck and Fard uncovered,

04:00.980 --> 04:06.060
we first need to pull back the curtain on how researchers actually measure what an AI is thinking in the first place.

04:06.120 --> 04:07.600
Right, because it's not like reading a book.

04:07.740 --> 04:08.840
No, not at all.

04:08.840 --> 04:13.580
When you type a prompt into an AI, let's say a translation request,

04:13.960 --> 04:16.480
it doesn't just instantly spit out the French equivalent.

04:17.020 --> 04:24.120
The prompt is broken down into numbers and it passes through dozens, sometimes hundreds of layers of artificial neurons.

04:24.440 --> 04:29.300
As it passes through these layers, the model generates internal hidden representations.

04:29.760 --> 04:32.560
These are often called hidden states or latent states, right?

04:32.560 --> 04:39.300
I always imagine them as high-dimensional coordinates, like a point floating in a space with thousands of dimensions.

04:39.800 --> 04:41.440
That's exactly what they are mathematically.

04:42.020 --> 04:45.040
A single hidden state is just a massive list of numbers,

04:45.340 --> 04:51.860
a vector that represents the AI's current understanding of the prompt at that specific layer of computation.

04:52.260 --> 04:57.040
So what do researchers do when they want to peer inside the black box?

04:57.240 --> 05:00.980
They train smaller, separate machine learning models called probes.

05:00.980 --> 05:05.140
They attach these probes to the hidden layers to try and decode those vectors.

05:05.820 --> 05:12.360
They are essentially asking, hey, at layer 40 out of 100, what information is currently residing in this vector space?

05:12.440 --> 05:13.200
They're taking the x-ray.

05:13.560 --> 05:14.720
Exactly, they take the x-ray.

05:15.120 --> 05:22.040
So Sinek and Fard were running these probes on massive models trying to map out the AI's internal thought process step by step.

05:22.060 --> 05:22.700
And what did they find?

05:23.020 --> 05:28.720
They found an incredible, baffling anomaly regarding how the models hold on to identity.

05:28.720 --> 05:35.920
They discovered that what they termed task-level identity is preserved beautifully throughout the entire network.

05:36.360 --> 05:37.740
Okay, but what about the rest of it?

05:37.820 --> 05:38.440
Well, that's the thing.

05:38.520 --> 05:41.600
The instance-level identity completely collapses and vanishes.

05:41.680 --> 05:42.760
Okay, we need to ground that.

05:42.880 --> 05:45.800
What do we mean by task-level versus instance-level?

05:46.000 --> 05:47.240
Give me a concrete example.

05:47.420 --> 05:48.900
Let's stick with the translation example.

05:49.460 --> 05:54.320
Let's say you ask an AI to translate a very specific sentence from English to French.

05:54.540 --> 05:57.200
Let's use, the orange cat sat on the blue mat.

05:57.200 --> 06:00.940
In this scenario, the task is the broad category of the work.

06:01.300 --> 06:02.420
The task is translation.

06:02.820 --> 06:03.300
Makes sense.

06:03.440 --> 06:07.080
The instance is the highly specific detailed problem you want solved.

06:07.580 --> 06:10.200
The instance is translating those specific words.

06:10.880 --> 06:12.640
The orange cat sat on the blue mat.

06:13.060 --> 06:13.440
Got it.

06:13.620 --> 06:17.140
Task is the genre, instance is the specific script.

06:17.320 --> 06:17.780
Precisely.

06:17.780 --> 06:31.940
Now, Cetic and Fard found that if you pause the AI right in the middle of its deep neural processing, say, at layer 50, and you use a probe to look at its internal state, you can clearly definitively see that the AI knows it is doing a translation task.

06:32.240 --> 06:34.000
So the probe lights up for translation.

06:34.000 --> 06:40.220
Yes, the probe will light up and say, yes, this vector is deep in the region of the brain dedicated to language translation.

06:40.740 --> 06:45.360
It hasn't confused its job with doing a calculus equation or writing Python code.

06:45.580 --> 06:48.700
The task-level identity is perfectly robustly stable.

06:48.860 --> 06:52.200
Yes, but here is the bizarre mind-bending part.

06:52.200 --> 07:00.940
When they took a probe and tried to interrogate that exact same internal state to find out which specific sentence it was currently translating.

07:01.140 --> 07:03.600
Like whether it was the cat sentence or a totally different one.

07:03.660 --> 07:04.060
Exactly.

07:04.260 --> 07:10.740
Whether it was the orange cat sat on the blue mat or a totally different sentence like the loud dog barked at the mailman.

07:11.100 --> 07:13.580
The probe found absolutely nothing.

07:13.700 --> 07:14.260
Wait, nothing?

07:14.520 --> 07:14.780
Nothing.

07:15.260 --> 07:17.300
The instance-level identity had collapsed.

07:17.300 --> 07:26.460
To the measuring tools, all the distinct, highly specific translation problems had just merged together into this generic, blurry, I am translating something, but I don't know what state.

07:26.740 --> 07:27.060
Wow.

07:27.200 --> 07:31.980
The detailed geometry that separated the cat sentence from the dog sentence had just vanished.

07:32.340 --> 07:35.000
It's like the AI walks into a room to get something.

07:35.720 --> 07:39.060
It perfectly remembers that it's in the kitchen to get food.

07:39.580 --> 07:40.600
That's the task.

07:40.940 --> 07:45.420
But it has completely, totally forgotten if it came in for an apple or a turkey sandwich.

07:45.600 --> 07:46.620
That's exactly it.

07:46.620 --> 07:51.000
But then it somehow still walks out of the kitchen with the exact turkey sandwich you asked for.

07:51.200 --> 07:55.920
It's a wonderful analogy because it highlights the absurdity of the situation.

07:56.240 --> 08:02.760
If you look at the AI in the middle of the process, it appears to have amnesia regarding your specific prompt.

08:02.980 --> 08:08.800
Yet at the final layer, it outputs the correct French words for orange cat and blue mat.

08:09.000 --> 08:11.160
This would drive researchers insane.

08:11.160 --> 08:14.840
I mean, if the details aren't in the hidden state, how can it solve the problem?

08:15.320 --> 08:18.040
So how did Setic and Fard try to explain this?

08:18.140 --> 08:21.260
Well, they attempted to build a rigorous foundation to study it.

08:21.660 --> 08:25.700
They realized that the field of AI interpretability was a bit of the Wild West.

08:26.220 --> 08:27.960
Everyone was defining a thought differently.

08:28.160 --> 08:29.080
So they needed rules.

08:29.080 --> 08:29.820
Exactly.

08:30.460 --> 08:33.680
So Setic and Fard proposed a foundational framework.

08:34.060 --> 08:36.640
They laid out four axioms of thought.

08:37.400 --> 08:45.940
They wanted to create an ironclad mathematical baseline to define what a valid thought representation inside an AI should actually look like.

08:46.040 --> 08:46.800
And what were those four?

08:46.800 --> 08:52.700
They named these four pillars causality, minimality, separability, and stability.

08:53.020 --> 08:59.580
I want to spend a minute here because to understand why the affliction paper is so disruptive, we have to understand what it's tearing down.

08:59.780 --> 08:59.900
Right.

08:59.980 --> 09:02.200
What is the logic behind these four axioms?

09:02.280 --> 09:02.400
Right.

09:02.600 --> 09:08.180
Why did the entire AI community look at Setic and Fard's framework and nod their heads in agreement?

09:08.440 --> 09:11.420
Because on their face, they make perfect intuitive sense.

09:11.600 --> 09:13.780
They align with how we naturally think about information.

09:13.940 --> 09:14.980
Let's take them one by one.

09:14.980 --> 09:16.380
First is causality.

09:16.380 --> 09:16.780
Okay.

09:16.860 --> 09:18.020
What does that mean in this context?

09:18.500 --> 09:26.400
This axiom states that if you identify a point in the AI's network that you believe is a thought, that point must causally affect the final output.

09:26.580 --> 09:27.360
So it has to do something.

09:27.780 --> 09:28.100
Yes.

09:28.700 --> 09:36.760
If researchers artificially reach into the network and delete or alter that specific vector, the AI's final answer should change.

09:37.300 --> 09:41.800
If changing the vector does nothing, then that vector wasn't actually a functional thought.

09:42.160 --> 09:43.120
That makes total sense.

09:43.120 --> 09:44.980
I mean, a thought has to have an effect.

09:44.980 --> 09:48.020
If I think about moving my arm, my arm moves.

09:48.140 --> 09:48.240
Right.

09:48.300 --> 09:50.360
If I change the thought, the arm does something else.

09:50.500 --> 09:50.940
Exactly.

09:51.720 --> 09:53.520
The second axiom is minimality.

09:54.100 --> 09:55.480
This deals with efficiency.

09:56.140 --> 10:05.980
It states that a true thought representation should strip away useless noise and only carry the minimum necessary information required from the input prompt to complete the task.

10:05.980 --> 10:08.580
It shouldn't be bogged down with irrelevant data.

10:08.680 --> 10:09.080
Got it.

10:09.340 --> 10:10.100
What about the third one?

10:10.500 --> 10:10.900
Separability.

10:11.360 --> 10:12.420
Separability is crucial.

10:12.940 --> 10:23.320
It states that if you have two fundamentally different thoughts, like our two different sentences, the cat and the dog, you should be able to mathematically measure a clear distinct distance between them in the AI's geometric space.

10:23.320 --> 10:24.300
So they shouldn't overlap.

10:24.540 --> 10:24.740
Right.

10:25.000 --> 10:29.240
If they are different concepts, they should be located in different areas of the vector space.

10:29.360 --> 10:30.180
They must be separable.

10:30.580 --> 10:34.200
Which is exactly what they weren't seeing with the instance level identity.

10:34.840 --> 10:37.920
The cat sentence and dog sentence were literally right on top of each other.

10:38.020 --> 10:38.460
Precisely.

10:38.460 --> 10:40.740
They were violating the axiom of separability.

10:41.360 --> 10:43.540
And finally, the fourth axiom is stability.

10:44.300 --> 10:52.020
This suggests that if you have two very similar thoughts, they should stay close to each other in the model's geometric space over time.

10:52.140 --> 10:57.960
Meaning small changes in the input shouldn't cause massive chaotic leaps in the internal representation.

10:58.380 --> 10:58.780
Exactly.

10:58.980 --> 10:59.260
Okay.

10:59.460 --> 11:01.440
I see why this was widely accepted.

11:01.820 --> 11:05.540
It's a very logical, almost Newtonian way of looking at AI.

11:05.540 --> 11:12.240
You measure a point, you see if it causes an action, you measure its distance from other points to ensure they are distinct.

11:12.620 --> 11:13.320
It's very clean.

11:14.160 --> 11:22.540
So they build this massive theoretical framework, but they still have this glaring anomaly where the AI violates separability by forgetting the specific sentence.

11:22.960 --> 11:25.220
Where does the new Flyxion paper come in?

11:25.520 --> 11:28.320
How do they disrupt this very neat logical framework?

11:28.320 --> 11:35.460
The Flyxion paper steps back, looks at those four axioms, and essentially drops a philosophical bomb on the entire premise.

11:35.800 --> 11:44.660
The authors point out a massive underlying unexamined assumption that Setic Fard and, frankly, the entire field of AI interpretability were making.

11:45.160 --> 11:51.640
They point out that all four of those axioms, causality, minimality, separability, and stability, share one fatal flaw.

11:51.760 --> 11:52.380
What's the flaw?

11:52.380 --> 12:01.060
They all implicitly assume that a thought is a location, a state, a single static point in high-dimensional geometric space.

12:01.120 --> 12:02.060
We assume it's an x-ray.

12:02.280 --> 12:02.840
Exactly.

12:03.380 --> 12:07.500
The Flyxion paper argues this is a profound ontological misalignment.

12:07.900 --> 12:12.760
Ontological, meaning the study of the nature of being, what a thing actually is.

12:12.760 --> 12:13.040
Yes.

12:13.340 --> 12:18.100
The Flyxion authors are stating you are looking at the fundamental nature of a computational thought incorrectly.

12:18.640 --> 12:29.180
If your ontology dictates that a thought is a single point in space, you will naturally become deeply confused when you inspect that single point and discover it doesn't maintain all the information you think it should.

12:29.520 --> 12:32.680
Like the specific granular details of the user's prompt.

12:32.680 --> 12:41.140
Okay, I have to jump in here and push back on this because I want to make sure I am truly grasping this and I suspect you listening might be wondering the same thing.

12:42.160 --> 12:43.800
Let's say the Flyxion paper is right.

12:44.340 --> 12:45.960
Let's say a thought isn't a point.

12:46.480 --> 12:49.360
But I am still hung up on the turkey sandwich in the kitchen.

12:49.560 --> 12:50.040
Fair enough.

12:50.040 --> 13:05.060
If the AI model demonstrably loses the specific details of my prompt in its internal state, if that information is genuinely missing from the snapshot we take, how does it ever, ever arrive at the correct French sentence at the end?

13:05.320 --> 13:06.880
That is the big question.

13:07.020 --> 13:08.340
I'm trying to visualize this.

13:08.340 --> 13:24.200
It sounds like you are trying to figure out if someone took the busy interstate highway or the quiet scenic back roads to get to a destination, but the only evidence you are allowed to look at is the fact that they are currently standing in aisle four of a grocery store looking at cereal.

13:24.420 --> 13:24.840
Right.

13:25.960 --> 13:31.040
You can't possibly tell how they got to the store just by looking at them standing there.

13:31.280 --> 13:35.720
If you only look at the snapshot, the information of the journey is completely invisible.

13:35.720 --> 13:39.040
You're demanding I figure out the plot of the movie from a single frame.

13:39.320 --> 13:41.240
That is a phenomenal way to articulate it.

13:41.600 --> 13:47.740
You've just hit on the exact friction point the researchers highlighted, and you've perfectly set up the core thesis of the Flyxion paper.

13:48.520 --> 13:55.760
What's fascinating here is what they are arguing is that reasoning is fiercely, fundamentally path-dependent.

13:55.820 --> 14:03.960
Path-dependent, meaning the journey, the specific route taken matters just as much, if not entirely more, than the current location you are standing in.

14:04.020 --> 14:04.500
Exactly.

14:04.500 --> 14:14.700
A single latent state, that static snapshot of the person in the cereal aisle, or that vector floating in layer 50 of the neural network, cannot by definition tell you the trajectory.

14:14.980 --> 14:15.220
Okay.

14:15.440 --> 14:17.400
It's a principle analogous to physics.

14:18.060 --> 14:25.100
Knowing a particle's exact precise position in space doesn't tell you its momentum, its velocity, or where it's going next.

14:25.140 --> 14:25.500
Oh, wow.

14:25.600 --> 14:29.240
So the Flyxion paper is saying SEDIC and FARD's empirical findings aren't a glitch.

14:29.240 --> 14:40.300
Right. This weird forgetting of the specific instance-level prompt isn't a glitch in the AI. It isn't a failure of the neural network's memory. It is a failure of our human measurement tools.

14:40.440 --> 14:47.620
We are stubbornly trying to force a dynamic, sequentially evolving moving process into a static, point-based ontology.

14:47.940 --> 14:52.360
That is exactly it. We are looking for an object when we should be measuring a movement.

14:52.360 --> 15:22.340
That's exactly right.

15:22.340 --> 15:24.540
You don't. You are at the destination or you aren't.

15:24.640 --> 15:25.000
Yes.

15:25.300 --> 15:32.380
But what this paper is arguing at a mathematical level is that reasoning isn't a light bulb turning on in an empty room.

15:32.380 --> 15:37.160
It's a continuous flowing movement. It is the act of traversing a space.

15:37.280 --> 15:43.540
That is the exact philosophical shift the paper demands of the reader. It requires abandoning the comfort of static points.

15:43.620 --> 15:47.260
Okay. So I am willing to take the leap. I will abandon the x-ray.

15:47.260 --> 15:55.700
But if a thought isn't a single point on a map, what exactly is it? And more importantly, what is it moving through? Where is this movement actually happening?

15:55.700 --> 16:03.100
And that brings us to the new mathematical framework the Flyxion authors introduce to replace Sedeck and Fard's axioms.

16:03.760 --> 16:07.320
They introduce a concept they call the admissibility field.

16:07.600 --> 16:09.260
Okay. The admissibility field.

16:09.360 --> 16:13.320
Yes. This is formally introduced as definition one in the paper.

16:13.980 --> 16:17.780
The admissibility field, which they represent with a capital letter A.

16:18.120 --> 16:22.900
This is the environment in which the dynamic trajectory of thought actually takes place.

16:22.900 --> 16:27.020
Let's break this down carefully. In the paper, the mathematical definition is dense.

16:27.940 --> 16:33.900
It says the admissibility field is a set-valued map determined by a decoding distribution and a threshold.

16:34.140 --> 16:35.120
I know. It's a lot.

16:35.460 --> 16:40.280
Yeah. I'm going to need you to translate that from theoretical math speak into something I can picture in my head.

16:40.580 --> 16:41.020
Happily.

16:41.160 --> 16:41.340
Yeah.

16:41.760 --> 16:46.600
Let's step away from the math for a second and look at how a model actually generates text.

16:46.900 --> 16:50.720
When you give an AI a prompt, it doesn't just think of the one perfect next word.

16:50.840 --> 16:51.740
Right. It has options.

16:51.740 --> 17:00.980
Exactly. It looks at its entire vocabulary, tens of thousands of words or tokens, and it assigns a probability score to every single one of them based on the context of your prompt.

17:01.120 --> 17:01.940
Okay. I track that.

17:02.200 --> 17:08.040
Now, based on that prompt, there are mathematically infinite ways the AI could combine words to respond.

17:08.340 --> 17:16.960
But there is a much, much smaller set of words that actually make logical, grammatical, and statistical sense as the very next step.

17:17.080 --> 17:18.220
Okay. So it narrows it down.

17:18.220 --> 17:27.440
Right. The admissibility field is essentially the map of all those statistically valid next steps a model could possibly take at any given moment in its computation.

17:27.920 --> 17:30.220
It is the boundary of what is permissible.

17:30.220 --> 17:34.180
So it's not looking at the one single word the AI eventually chooses to output.

17:34.560 --> 17:42.320
It's looking at the entire universe of words the AI considered choosing, but specifically the ones that passed a certain threshold of making sense.

17:42.520 --> 17:43.040
Precisely.

17:43.300 --> 17:45.800
Let's say the prompt you gave the AI is simply,

17:45.800 --> 17:49.000
the color of the clear daytime sky is.

17:49.280 --> 17:49.520
Okay.

17:49.700 --> 17:54.920
At that exact moment, the words blue, azure, or bright might all have high probability scores.

17:55.180 --> 17:55.660
Mm-hmm.

17:55.660 --> 17:56.780
They cross the threshold.

17:57.180 --> 17:59.240
They exist within the admissibility field.

17:59.360 --> 18:00.740
But the word refrigerator wouldn't.

18:00.940 --> 18:01.400
Exactly.

18:01.700 --> 18:06.400
The word refrigerator or the word running or the word 17 would have near zero probability.

18:06.600 --> 18:07.820
They do not cross the threshold.

18:08.000 --> 18:09.820
They fall outside the admissibility field.

18:09.940 --> 18:10.220
Got it.

18:10.220 --> 18:14.620
The field represents the strict boundaries of logical statistical continuation.

18:15.200 --> 18:19.740
It is the shape of all possible correct or at least coherent futures.

18:20.520 --> 18:21.040
Okay.

18:21.100 --> 18:23.280
I want to play with this because I need a strong visual.

18:23.600 --> 18:32.360
I'm picturing this admissibility field not as a flat map, but as a three-dimensional landscape, like a massive river basin surrounded by mountains.

18:32.500 --> 18:33.360
Oh, I like that.

18:33.420 --> 18:38.740
When we give the AI that initial input prompt, it's like tectonic plates shifting beneath the earth.

18:38.880 --> 18:39.180
Yeah.

18:39.180 --> 18:41.720
The specific words of the prompt create the mountains.

18:41.940 --> 18:43.040
They carve out the valleys.

18:43.260 --> 18:47.120
They establish the structural constraints and the topology of the terrain.

18:47.300 --> 18:48.260
Beautiful analogy.

18:48.760 --> 18:53.700
And the thought, the actual sequence of internal states and eventual words the AI generates.

18:53.880 --> 18:57.400
It's just the water flowing down the paths of least resistance through that basin.

18:57.680 --> 18:58.120
Yes.

18:58.380 --> 18:59.900
The water can take different channels.

19:00.040 --> 19:05.140
It can swirl in eddies, but it is entirely constrained by the shape of the basin that the prompt created.

19:05.420 --> 19:07.780
That is an exceptionally powerful visualization.

19:07.780 --> 19:10.900
The prompt dictates the topology of the field.

19:11.400 --> 19:19.880
And the reasoning process, the thought, is the actual physical trajectory of the water navigating that specific constrained topology over time.

19:19.880 --> 19:25.540
Okay, if we accept this river basin model, how does this solve the core mystery?

19:26.080 --> 19:28.580
How does this explain the collapsing instance identity?

19:29.180 --> 19:33.000
Why does the AI seem to forget the specific sentence it is translating?

19:33.460 --> 19:39.060
Why does it lose sight of the specific orange cat and blue mat as the water flows down the river?

19:39.060 --> 19:45.160
This is where the paper introduces a vital mathematical proof called Proposition 2 State Trajectory Non-Equivalence.

19:45.300 --> 19:46.900
State Trajectory Non-Equivalence.

19:46.940 --> 19:52.820
It sounds intimidating, but it actually proves something very intuitive and it maps perfectly onto your river analogy.

19:53.080 --> 20:00.260
The authors mathematically prove that the mapping from trajectories, the path, to terminal states, the final destination, is what they call many-to-one.

20:00.680 --> 20:01.320
Many-to-one.

20:01.600 --> 20:06.120
Meaning many different unique paths can all lead to the exact same final destination.

20:06.120 --> 20:08.500
Exactly. Think about your river basin.

20:08.780 --> 20:14.740
You could have a raindrop fall on the extreme north ridge and it winds its way down a specific gully.

20:14.920 --> 20:21.220
You could have another raindrop fall on the eastern slope and it takes a completely different path through a different canyon.

20:21.400 --> 20:23.400
Right, they're completely different trajectories.

20:23.700 --> 20:27.720
But eventually they both flow into the exact same lake at the bottom of the basin.

20:28.060 --> 20:28.540
I see.

20:28.700 --> 20:33.160
If you are a researcher and you only look at the water once it is already in the lake,

20:33.160 --> 20:39.080
if you only look at the terminal state or a deep latent state near the end of the neural network,

20:40.260 --> 20:45.460
you cannot possibly reverse engineer the specific journey that water took from the top of the mountain.

20:45.920 --> 20:46.240
Wow.

20:46.820 --> 20:52.520
The geometric information about the specific path is fundamentally lost if you only measure the destination point.

20:52.660 --> 20:53.140
Exactly.

20:53.500 --> 21:01.400
This actually solves another really weird nagging finding from the original SEDIC and FARD paper that was driving me crazy when I read the background material.

21:01.400 --> 21:03.060
I wrote the input embedding finding.

21:03.480 --> 21:03.760
Yes.

21:04.720 --> 21:11.560
SEDIC and FARD noted that the very first initial input prompt the embedding right at the start of the AI's process

21:11.560 --> 21:17.780
held vastly more measurable, recoverable information about the specific granular details of the problem

21:17.780 --> 21:21.160
than the model's actual internal thoughts later on in the process.

21:21.260 --> 21:21.580
Right.

21:21.900 --> 21:22.640
Seems backwards.

21:22.640 --> 21:23.760
It totally does.

21:23.920 --> 21:24.040
Yeah.

21:24.080 --> 21:32.580
When I first read that under the old X-ray snapshot theory that made zero sense to me, I thought, wait, more computation should mean more information is being generated, right?

21:32.720 --> 21:38.660
The longer the AI thinks, the richer and more detailed its internal representation should become.

21:39.040 --> 21:43.300
It felt like they were saying the AI gets dumber and more forgetful the longer it calculates.

21:43.360 --> 21:43.560
Right.

21:43.640 --> 21:46.680
And your confusion is completely justified under a state-based view.

21:46.680 --> 21:54.180
If you assume a thought is a single location that you are building and refining, then yes, more computation should equal a more detailed, richer state.

21:54.380 --> 21:55.680
But under this new view...

21:55.680 --> 22:00.700
Under the trajectory view proposed by Flix-Yearn, this phenomenon is not a paradox at all.

22:00.960 --> 22:02.660
It is a mathematical necessity.

22:02.660 --> 22:08.100
They explain exactly why the early layers hold more specific information than the later layers.

22:08.480 --> 22:13.880
Because the initial prompt embedding the very first step defines the entire admissibility field.

22:14.300 --> 22:17.740
It defines the entire river basin, every mountain, and every valley.

22:18.320 --> 22:19.780
It has to hold all constraints.

22:20.140 --> 22:21.980
The prompt is the tectonic event.

22:22.500 --> 22:24.800
It holds the blueprint for the entire landscape.

22:24.940 --> 22:25.220
Yes.

22:25.560 --> 22:28.460
It perfectly anchors the initial boundary conditions.

22:28.460 --> 22:38.360
But as the model begins to compute, as it passes through the layers, as the water begins to flow, it is moving along one specific path driven by a powerful force.

22:38.560 --> 22:39.020
Which is?

22:39.600 --> 22:40.120
Optimization.

22:40.560 --> 22:44.740
The network is trying to get to the correct answer as efficiently as possible.

22:45.080 --> 22:55.760
When you measure a later state deep in the network, it yields less specific information about the initial prompt because the traversal is physically moving further away from those initial boundary conditions.

22:55.920 --> 22:57.040
It is hurtling toward the lake.

22:57.040 --> 22:58.360
It is hurtling toward the lake.

22:58.460 --> 23:14.120
The network is optimizing for the global trajectory, making sure the water gets down the mountain safely and grammatically, rather than wasting its limited computational energy, maintaining a perfect high-resolution memory of exactly which snowflake on which peak started the avalanche.

23:14.340 --> 23:15.420
So what does this all mean?

23:15.520 --> 23:16.880
Let me make sure I have this locked in.

23:17.280 --> 23:27.660
What this means is that the river of thought intentionally loses the specific instance-level details of its origin source as it flows because it simply doesn't need them to keep flowing downhill.

23:27.660 --> 23:28.660
You've got it.

23:28.880 --> 23:32.020
The topology of the base and the task guides it naturally.

23:32.780 --> 23:38.460
It is optimizing for the journey for the flow, not for hoarding useless details about the starting line.

23:38.540 --> 23:38.940
Exactly.

23:39.080 --> 23:40.900
It's a profound paradigm shift.

23:41.480 --> 23:45.200
This forgetting is not a failure of the AI's memory architecture.

23:45.640 --> 23:51.320
It is a fundamental feature of efficient, constrained traversal through a complex probability field.

23:51.400 --> 23:51.700
But wait.

23:52.120 --> 23:53.460
This brings up a huge question.

23:53.700 --> 23:54.020
Why?

23:54.020 --> 23:55.240
Why does it happen?

23:55.440 --> 23:55.560
Yeah.

23:55.640 --> 23:58.840
Why does the training process of the AI force it to behave this way?

23:59.200 --> 24:03.820
I get that it's efficient, but these models have billions, sometimes trillions of parameters.

24:04.140 --> 24:05.620
They have massive amounts of memory.

24:06.060 --> 24:12.660
Why can't the AI just carry all that granular information, every blade of grass, every detail of the prompt, all the way down the river?

24:13.040 --> 24:14.580
Why does it actively erase it?

24:14.580 --> 24:19.300
To answer that, the Fluxena paper pulls from an entirely different branch of science.

24:20.080 --> 24:25.080
They bring in statistical physics, specifically a concept called renormalization.

24:25.600 --> 24:26.280
Renormalization.

24:26.280 --> 24:30.200
The physics influence in this section of the paper is incredibly elegant.

24:30.880 --> 24:36.020
The authors introduced two crucial new definitions to explain the mechanism of this erasure.

24:36.920 --> 24:42.280
Definition three is the renormalization curve, denoted mathematically as R of epsilon.

24:42.840 --> 24:47.280
And definition four is the critical scale, denoted as epsilon sub c.

24:47.600 --> 24:47.860
Okay.

24:48.040 --> 24:51.480
Let's take these one at a time, because we are getting to the deep end of the pool here.

24:51.680 --> 24:54.760
Let's start with the concept of renormalization itself.

24:55.200 --> 24:57.940
What is that, and how does it apply to a language model?

24:58.520 --> 25:03.100
Renormalization is a concept borrowed directly from statistical mechanics and quantum field theory.

25:03.220 --> 25:03.680
Casual.

25:03.820 --> 25:04.020
Right.

25:04.200 --> 25:04.960
Just light reading.

25:05.340 --> 25:09.220
In physics, it describes how you look at a complex system at different scales of resolution,

25:09.520 --> 25:13.880
and how the behavior of that system changes depending on how far you zoom in or zoom out.

25:13.960 --> 25:16.620
Like looking at something under a microscope versus from an airplane.

25:17.000 --> 25:17.440
Exactly.

25:17.860 --> 25:19.700
Think about studying a cup of water.

25:19.700 --> 25:29.200
If you zoom all the way in to the subatomic scale, you have to track the chaotic high energy interactions of billions of individual quantum particles.

25:29.440 --> 25:31.100
It's overwhelmingly complex.

25:31.240 --> 25:31.900
Way too much data.

25:32.160 --> 25:37.240
But if you zoom out to the macroscopic scale, all those chaotic quantum fluctuations average out,

25:37.320 --> 25:41.000
and you just get the smooth, predictable laws of fluid dynamics.

25:41.360 --> 25:42.100
Water flows.

25:42.600 --> 25:45.420
The system has been renormalized at a larger scale.

25:45.580 --> 25:45.920
I see.

25:45.920 --> 25:53.820
The Flyxion paper argues that the training process of a large language model acts exactly like a physical renormalization process.

25:53.980 --> 25:58.660
So training an AI on billions of words is like zooming out on a physical system.

25:58.840 --> 25:59.400
Precisely.

25:59.660 --> 26:05.020
Think of the optimization process, the actual training where the AI reads the internet and updates its weights,

26:05.380 --> 26:07.840
acting on a system that has limited finite capacity.

26:07.840 --> 26:12.940
Despite having billions of parameters, an AI does not have infinite memory.

26:13.180 --> 26:15.120
It cannot perfectly memorize the entire internet.

26:15.320 --> 26:16.300
So it has to make choices.

26:16.660 --> 26:16.860
Right.

26:17.540 --> 26:22.940
So the Brutal mathematics of training applies a renormalization operator to the network.

26:23.340 --> 26:31.240
It essentially forces the AI to group concepts together based on a specific scale of resolution which they represent with the Greek letter epsilon.

26:31.240 --> 26:36.200
It's forcing the AI to compress information because it only has so much space in its digital brain.

26:36.360 --> 26:38.500
It has to choose what matters and what doesn't.

26:38.700 --> 26:39.000
Exactly.

26:39.460 --> 26:42.280
And that brings us to the critical scale epsilon sub c.

26:43.080 --> 26:48.060
The critical scale is the exact mathematical threshold, the specific resolution level,

26:48.280 --> 26:52.280
where details either survive the compression process or vanish completely.

26:52.460 --> 26:52.900
Oh, wow.

26:52.900 --> 26:57.860
If a piece of information, a specific detail, is functionally above the critical scale,

26:57.980 --> 27:02.860
the AI deems it important enough to dedicate permanent memory to it, it remembers it.

27:02.920 --> 27:03.780
And if it's below?

27:03.940 --> 27:07.120
If a detail falls below the critical scale, the AI essentially says,

27:07.240 --> 27:11.260
I cannot afford to store this, and it integrates it out, it averages it away, it erases it.

27:11.380 --> 27:14.780
Okay, so this is the million-dollar question for understanding AI behavior.

27:15.420 --> 27:18.860
What exactly determines what gets erased and what gets saved?

27:19.460 --> 27:21.780
Who or what sets the critical scale?

27:21.780 --> 27:27.380
What is the actual mechanism of erasure happening inside the neural network during training?

27:27.880 --> 27:31.460
The mechanism is entirely driven by variance in the training signal,

27:31.660 --> 27:35.800
which mathematically translates to minimizing expected action cost.

27:35.920 --> 27:37.320
Okay, I need you to break that down.

27:37.800 --> 27:40.120
What do you mean by variance in the training signal?

27:40.460 --> 27:42.440
Explain it like I'm a student in your class.

27:42.620 --> 27:45.740
Let's imagine you are a teacher grading a million essays.

27:46.160 --> 27:47.540
That is the training process.

27:47.760 --> 27:48.580
Sounds exhausting.

27:48.780 --> 27:49.220
It is.

27:49.220 --> 27:54.180
When an AI is training, it is constantly trying to minimize the cost of its actions,

27:54.720 --> 27:59.100
meaning it wants to adjust its internal weight so that it gets the right answer more often,

27:59.280 --> 28:01.860
thereby getting a lower error score or cost.

28:01.960 --> 28:02.700
Okay, makes sense.

28:02.900 --> 28:05.920
Now consider course high-level task-level distinctions.

28:06.460 --> 28:11.280
For example, the fundamental difference between solving a math equation and translating a sentence.

28:11.440 --> 28:12.580
Two totally different things.

28:12.580 --> 28:13.140
Right.

28:13.140 --> 28:17.120
Across the billions of documents the AI reads during training,

28:17.700 --> 28:23.900
the linguistic structure of math and the linguistic structure of translation are vastly different and wildly consistent.

28:24.100 --> 28:25.220
Math always looks like math.

28:25.220 --> 28:35.320
Yes, because it is so consistent, the gradient signals the mathematical nudges telling the AI how to adjust its brain are incredibly strong and unified.

28:35.820 --> 28:43.580
The AI gets a massive consistent reward for learning and dedicating architecture to the difference between math and language.

28:43.720 --> 28:44.120
I see.

28:44.120 --> 28:50.040
Because the variance is low and the reward is high, that distinction stays safely above the critical scale.

28:50.340 --> 28:52.680
The model builds a permanent structure for it.

28:52.840 --> 28:53.720
Okay, that makes sense.

28:53.780 --> 28:56.020
It's a high-value, highly repetitive piece of information.

28:56.260 --> 28:58.720
It's easy to learn because the rule rarely changes.

28:58.940 --> 28:59.240
Yes.

28:59.800 --> 29:05.420
But now let's look at highly specific instance-level distinctions, the tiny details.

29:05.620 --> 29:07.440
Let's look at the difference between translating the sentence,

29:07.580 --> 29:09.940
the orange cat sat, versus translating the sentence,

29:10.240 --> 29:11.200
the brown dog slept.

29:11.200 --> 29:13.920
Across the billions of documents on the internet,

29:14.460 --> 29:18.480
the contextual rules governing those highly specific phrases are chaotic.

29:19.200 --> 29:23.660
They appear in wildly different contexts, surrounded by different words with different syntax.

29:23.780 --> 29:26.360
Right, there's no universal rule for when a brown dog sleeps.

29:26.600 --> 29:26.960
Exactly.

29:27.520 --> 29:32.580
The training signals for those specific tiny differences have incredibly high variance.

29:33.000 --> 29:33.740
They are noisy.

29:34.160 --> 29:37.160
The mathematical nudges the AI receives are conflicting.

29:38.400 --> 29:39.260
Update this way.

29:39.400 --> 29:40.300
No, update that way.

29:40.300 --> 29:40.780
Oh, man.

29:40.860 --> 29:42.240
So the AI is just getting mixed signals.

29:42.420 --> 29:42.580
Yes.

29:42.940 --> 29:47.840
So the model's overarching optimization process, looking to minimize its error score

29:47.840 --> 29:52.000
while managing its finite parameter budget, essentially performs a calculation.

29:52.360 --> 29:54.280
It looks at its limited memory and says,

29:54.520 --> 29:57.300
this tiny high variance detail isn't worth my capacity.

29:57.500 --> 29:57.720
Wow.

29:57.940 --> 30:01.660
It says, I am not getting a consistent enough reward across my training data

30:01.660 --> 30:05.020
to permanently memorize the specific geometry of this exact sentence.

30:05.500 --> 30:06.640
And so it integrates it out.

30:06.740 --> 30:09.160
It drops the specific detail below the critical scale.

30:09.160 --> 30:11.680
It smooths over the noise to save space.

30:12.040 --> 30:14.680
Wait, but my ChatGPT works.

30:15.400 --> 30:22.480
If I log on right now and give ChatGPT a highly specific, totally bizarre prompt, like

30:22.480 --> 30:29.820
translate a sentence about a purple alien eating a taco on a surfboard into French, it does it perfectly.

30:29.920 --> 30:30.300
It does.

30:30.440 --> 30:32.540
It clearly didn't erase my specific prompt.

30:32.540 --> 30:33.840
It knows exactly what I asked.

30:34.340 --> 30:38.340
How do you square my everyday experience with this theory of aggressive erasure?

30:38.660 --> 30:43.700
Ah, this is the crucial distinction between the weights of the model and the activations of the model.

30:43.780 --> 30:43.940
Right.

30:43.940 --> 30:50.760
When we say the AI erases the detail, we mean it erases it from its permanent static topological map, its learned weights.

30:51.280 --> 30:55.640
It doesn't dedicate a permanent chunk of its architecture to the concept of alien eating a taco.

30:55.960 --> 30:59.540
That falls below the critical scale of its generalized understanding of the world.

30:59.680 --> 30:59.980
Got it.

30:59.980 --> 31:03.940
However, when you give it that prompt, it uses the high-level generalized rules.

31:04.260 --> 31:12.520
It did keep the task-level rules of French grammar and vocabulary, and it dynamically traverses the admissibility field to construct the answer on the fly.

31:12.640 --> 31:14.000
It's actively figuring it out.

31:14.220 --> 31:14.520
Right.

31:14.780 --> 31:22.700
It's the difference between memorizing the exact directions to every single house in a city versus just learning how to read a map and drive a car.

31:23.220 --> 31:24.820
The AI learns to drive.

31:24.940 --> 31:28.700
It drops the specific addresses below the critical scale.

31:28.700 --> 31:30.520
That is a fascinating distinction.

31:31.080 --> 31:31.220
Yeah.

31:31.320 --> 31:34.420
It is aggressively optimizing for generalization.

31:35.000 --> 31:38.820
You know, I'm realizing this is exactly like compressing a digital image.

31:38.960 --> 31:39.440
Oh, absolutely.

31:39.560 --> 31:42.400
I use this analogy sometimes when explaining digital media files.

31:43.000 --> 31:50.340
It's like turning a massive uncompressed raw photograph from a high-end camera into a tiny JPEG file you can text to a friend.

31:50.600 --> 31:51.020
Yes.

31:51.300 --> 31:53.620
When the software compresses the image, it looks at the pixels.

31:53.940 --> 31:56.540
It keeps the big macro shapes, so the sky is generally blue.

31:56.640 --> 31:58.260
The grass is generally green.

31:58.700 --> 32:00.380
That is the task-level identity.

32:00.560 --> 32:01.580
It survives the compression.

32:01.680 --> 32:02.820
Right, because it's coarse.

32:02.940 --> 32:07.960
But if you zoom in, the algorithm averages out the individual microscopic blades of grass.

32:07.960 --> 32:16.960
It blends them together into a block of green because storing the exact hexadecimal color code for every single blade takes up way too much hard drive space.

32:16.960 --> 32:27.080
The JPEG algorithm mathematically calculates that the individual blades of grass fall below the critical scale of what is necessary for a human eye to understand the basic picture.

32:27.080 --> 32:29.640
The JPEG comparison is incredibly apt.

32:29.640 --> 32:35.840
It perfectly illustrates the concept of resolution thresholds, but the Flyxion paper takes it one step further.

32:36.080 --> 32:36.460
How so?

32:36.460 --> 32:40.580
The AI compression isn't happening to a static file on a hard drive.

32:40.760 --> 32:46.160
It's happening to the model's dynamic behavioral tendencies over billions of training cycles.

32:46.560 --> 32:55.900
The optimization process of AI training acts as a continuous dynamic JPEG compression algorithm acting on the AI's internal representational space.

32:56.020 --> 32:56.280
Wow.

32:56.280 --> 33:00.660
And this brings us to what the paper calls the separability collapse corollary.

33:01.060 --> 33:03.880
This is where they close the loop on Setic and Fard.

33:04.320 --> 33:06.600
It is a brilliant piece of mathematical synthesis.

33:06.600 --> 33:14.320
The paper actually proves that the anomaly Setic and Fard found the vanishing cat and dog sentences isn't a glitch, but a mathematical certainty.

33:14.900 --> 33:22.560
Using the math of renormalization, they prove that Setic and Fard's empirical findings are just a natural, unavoidable theorem of this compression process.

33:22.620 --> 33:23.760
This has to work that way.

33:23.760 --> 33:30.600
Yes. Because the instance scale, the specific words, the blades of grass, has high variance and falls below the critical scale,

33:31.020 --> 33:38.720
the AI's internal ability to cleanly geometrically discriminate between those specific sentences drops to pure statistical chance.

33:38.840 --> 33:40.820
The space is mathematically smoothed out.

33:40.980 --> 33:41.260
Right.

33:41.520 --> 33:49.500
But because the task scale, the overall concept of translation of the blue sky and green grass, has low variance and remains safely above the critical scale,

33:49.500 --> 33:52.920
the model's ability to discriminate the task survives perfectly.

33:52.920 --> 33:56.200
So Setic and Fard weren't measuring a broken AI.

33:56.620 --> 33:58.600
The AI was operating flawlessly.

33:58.840 --> 33:59.020
Yeah.

33:59.340 --> 34:04.460
Our assumption that the AI should hoard every bleed of grass in its static memory is what was fundamentally broken.

34:04.520 --> 34:05.040
Precisely.

34:05.380 --> 34:10.940
The model is operating exactly as an optimized capacity limited system should in a complex environment.

34:11.320 --> 34:14.740
But this leaves us with a massive, glaring, lingering question.

34:14.820 --> 34:16.480
And this is where the detective story gets really good.

34:16.480 --> 34:16.760
Okay.

34:16.760 --> 34:32.460
If the find details the blades of grass, the specific instance identity, if they are compressed out of the permanent structure and they are missing from the individual points or states of thought as the AI computes, where on earth did the data go?

34:32.800 --> 34:32.980
Right.

34:33.060 --> 34:33.880
It has to be somewhere.

34:33.880 --> 34:37.300
Because the AI still successfully translates this specific sentence.

34:37.660 --> 34:45.200
The detail has to exist somewhere in the system during the computation for it to output the correct French words for purple alien and taco.

34:45.960 --> 34:52.780
If the data is not sitting in the static snapshot of layer 50, where is the memory hiding while the AI thinks?

34:52.780 --> 34:59.360
And that brings us to the core of the Flyxion Papers investigation, the four hypotheses of missing identity.

34:59.740 --> 34:59.820
Yes.

35:00.220 --> 35:03.440
The authors realize that the missing information must be accounted for.

35:04.060 --> 35:11.040
They lay out four distinct, mutually exclusive theoretical possibilities for where this missing instance identity could be hiding.

35:11.440 --> 35:15.460
And they systematically evaluate each one against the available evidence.

35:15.820 --> 35:17.300
Let's lay out the options they present.

35:17.420 --> 35:18.300
I want to go through all four.

35:18.380 --> 35:18.520
Sure.

35:18.960 --> 35:20.480
Hypothesis one is state encoding.

35:20.480 --> 35:22.720
This is the most conservative idea.

35:22.900 --> 35:28.680
This is the belief that the detail actually is hiding in that single static point in the layer 50 state.

35:29.160 --> 35:33.320
But our measuring tools, our linear probes, are just too weak or too blunt to see it.

35:33.780 --> 35:39.500
It's like trying to find a microscopic scratch on an x-ray using a magnifying glass instead of an electron microscope.

35:39.720 --> 35:39.860
Right.

35:40.060 --> 35:40.940
It's just a tool problem.

35:41.060 --> 35:41.500
Exactly.

35:41.900 --> 35:43.960
Hypothesis two is distributed state encoding.

35:44.320 --> 35:45.820
This is slightly more complex.

35:46.300 --> 35:50.120
This says the detail is in the state, but it's not in a simple readable format.

35:50.120 --> 35:58.800
It is scattered and hidden in some highly complex, tangled, non-linear geometry that our current mathematical tools simply cannot untangle.

35:58.900 --> 36:00.000
Still state-based, though.

36:00.080 --> 36:00.260
Yeah.

36:00.560 --> 36:01.460
Still in the snapshot.

36:02.200 --> 36:03.900
Hypothesis three is ensemble averaging.

36:04.500 --> 36:06.080
This moves away from the single point.

36:06.580 --> 36:10.500
This suggests that the answer isn't in one state, and you don't even need a specific path.

36:10.500 --> 36:13.560
You just need an unordered pile of all the states the AI went through.

36:14.080 --> 36:21.560
If you throw them all in a bucket and average them all together, the random noise cancels out, and the specific detail magically emerges from the aggregate.

36:22.280 --> 36:24.820
And finally, hypothesis four is trajectory encoding.

36:25.180 --> 36:26.880
This is the radical one.

36:26.980 --> 36:29.200
This is the core of the Flyxion paper.

36:29.200 --> 36:34.520
It argues that the identity is encoded purely in the temporal ordered structure of the path itself.

36:35.300 --> 36:36.580
The sequence is the message.

36:37.160 --> 36:39.940
The information exists in the movement, not in the locations.

36:40.380 --> 36:40.580
Right.

36:40.800 --> 36:44.280
Let's dissect the first two hypotheses, because they represent the old guard.

36:45.120 --> 36:49.400
Hypotheses one and two are essentially desperate attempts to save the state-based ontology.

36:49.920 --> 36:54.560
They deeply want the thought to still be a specific location, just one that is hard to read.

36:54.940 --> 36:56.140
They want the x-ray to work.

36:56.140 --> 36:56.700
Exactly.

36:57.320 --> 37:00.940
But the Flyxion authors point back to the source material to dismantle them.

37:01.660 --> 37:10.200
They point to a specific, rigorous ablation study done by Setic and Fard themselves, ablation D.8, in their original appendix.

37:10.360 --> 37:21.380
For our listeners who might not be deep into research methodology, an ablation study is basically where scientists systematically remove or isolate a variable in an experiment to see what breaks right.

37:21.700 --> 37:24.820
It's how you figure out what a specific part of a system actually does.

37:24.820 --> 37:25.700
Exactly right.

37:25.700 --> 37:30.280
In this specific ablation study, Setic and Fard wanted to test hypothesis one and two.

37:30.640 --> 37:33.360
They thought maybe our probes just suck, so they cranked up the power.

37:33.780 --> 37:38.760
They increased the mathematical capacity and complexity of their probing models by a full order of magnitude.

37:38.920 --> 37:40.520
They got the bigger magnifying glass.

37:40.720 --> 37:48.640
They went from using simple linear classifiers to highly complex nonlinear models designed to untangle deeply knotted data.

37:49.000 --> 37:50.900
They brought out the electron microscope.

37:51.100 --> 37:51.980
And what was the result?

37:51.980 --> 37:54.000
They still found absolutely nothing.

37:54.000 --> 38:03.960
The instance identity, the specific sentence, was completely and utterly unrecoverable from the single internal state, even with massively upgraded diagnostic tools.

38:03.960 --> 38:08.700
So H1 and H2 are severely on the ropes, if not entirely knocked out.

38:08.820 --> 38:13.280
The detail just isn't in the snapshot, no matter how powerful your magnifying glass is.

38:13.340 --> 38:14.660
The math says it's not there.

38:14.720 --> 38:15.160
Exactly.

38:15.300 --> 38:23.680
The empirical evidence heavily, heavily disfavors them, which leaves us with a fascinating showdown between hypothesis 3 and hypothesis 4.

38:23.680 --> 38:28.300
And I want to point out how philosophically divergent H3 and H4 are from each other.

38:28.460 --> 38:32.700
They represent two totally different views of how complex systems process information.

38:32.880 --> 38:33.340
They really do.

38:33.660 --> 38:38.920
H3 ensemble averaging basically says that the order of operations doesn't really matter.

38:39.320 --> 38:49.340
It says you can just grab a bucket, throw all the AI's intermediate thought states in there, willy-nilly shake it up, and the correct answer will eventually pop out because the statistical noise cancels itself out.

38:49.460 --> 38:49.640
Yeah.

38:49.820 --> 38:51.520
It's an unordered pile of data.

38:51.600 --> 38:51.780
Right.

38:51.780 --> 38:53.000
The order is irrelevant.

38:53.540 --> 38:57.740
But H4 trajectory encoding says the order is the only thing that matters.

38:57.940 --> 39:03.200
The specific step-by-step sequence through time is where the information actually lives.

39:03.400 --> 39:10.600
If you scramble the sequence, if you put step four before step two, you destroy the thought entirely, even if you have all the same pieces.

39:10.800 --> 39:12.720
That is the exact battleground.

39:12.860 --> 39:20.000
And if we connect this to the bigger picture of how we evaluate artificial intelligence moving forward, it fundamentally changes everything.

39:20.000 --> 39:24.080
We can no longer just check the AI's math at a single point in time.

39:24.200 --> 39:25.540
We can't rely on the X-ray.

39:26.120 --> 39:36.000
To figure out which hypothesis is right, whether human-like reasoning requires a bucket of unordered states or an ordered trajectory, we have to completely radically change our metrics of measurement.

39:36.540 --> 39:41.980
We have to stop measuring the geometric Euclidean distance between points in a static space.

39:41.980 --> 39:43.220
And start doing what?

39:43.700 --> 39:45.100
How do you measure a movement?

39:45.660 --> 39:48.080
We have to start measuring the distance between futures.

39:48.400 --> 39:52.620
I have to admit that sounds a little bit like science fiction, measuring the distance between futures.

39:52.800 --> 39:56.800
Let's get into the mechanics of this because this is where the math gets incredibly creative.

39:57.640 --> 39:59.960
How on earth do you measure a future?

40:00.500 --> 40:05.980
This is the core of the theoretical contribution in the paper, and it is theoretically gorgeous.

40:05.980 --> 40:18.840
The paper argues that because thoughts are trajectories through a probability space, you cannot use normal geometric distance, like taking a ruler and measuring the space between vector A and vector B.

40:19.040 --> 40:19.300
Okay.

40:19.480 --> 40:24.900
You have to use what they call continuation distribution distance, mathematically represented as D sub A.

40:25.020 --> 40:25.300
Okay.

40:25.600 --> 40:27.220
Continuation distribution distance.

40:27.340 --> 40:27.480
Yeah.

40:27.600 --> 40:29.320
Explain the logic behind that metric.

40:29.320 --> 40:30.540
The logic is this.

40:30.960 --> 40:38.740
We should judge two internal thoughts as being functionally equivalent if and only if the futures they probabilistically generate are identical.

40:39.220 --> 40:39.720
Right.

40:40.040 --> 40:45.400
We don't care if the geometric points look incredibly far apart in the model's internal brain map.

40:45.800 --> 40:55.700
If state A and state B both probabilistically lead the model to generate the exact same set of NEXT tokens, they are for all functional intents and purposes the exact same thought.

40:56.060 --> 40:57.140
They share a destination.

40:57.140 --> 40:58.460
I love this concept.

40:58.660 --> 41:01.800
Let me try an analogy here because I think I understand the shift in perspective.

41:02.120 --> 41:02.220
Yeah.

41:02.240 --> 41:03.020
Let's think about chess.

41:03.140 --> 41:03.340
Okay.

41:03.560 --> 41:03.840
Chess.

41:03.980 --> 41:07.700
Imagine we were comparing two different chess games being played by grandmasters.

41:08.240 --> 41:13.680
If we use the old method, the geometric state-based method, we look at the board right now, frozen in time.

41:14.240 --> 41:17.040
In game A, the white pawn is on E4.

41:17.620 --> 41:19.940
In game B, the white pawn is on E3.

41:20.720 --> 41:22.720
Geometrically, physically, the boards look different.

41:22.960 --> 41:23.240
Yes.

41:23.240 --> 41:27.780
So under the old rules, we would declare these are two entirely different game states.

41:27.900 --> 41:28.560
They are not the same.

41:28.640 --> 41:28.760
Right.

41:29.040 --> 41:34.300
But the Flyxion paper is saying, no, don't look at where the pieces physically are right now.

41:34.540 --> 41:36.020
Look at the futures they generate.

41:36.340 --> 41:38.040
Look at the probability space of the game.

41:38.500 --> 41:43.820
Look at the top five most likely NEXT moves that a grandmaster would make in both game A and game B.

41:43.820 --> 41:56.080
If game A and game B both mathematically force the exact same top five future moves, then strategically, functionally, they're fundamentally the exact same game state.

41:56.340 --> 42:02.100
Even if the board looks slightly different to the naked eye right now, the future they collapse into is identical.

42:02.100 --> 42:05.140
That is a phenomenal translation of the concept.

42:05.480 --> 42:09.380
You are comparing the continuation distributions, the set of likely NEXT moves.

42:09.520 --> 42:13.880
You are measuring the distance between the futures, not the current board states.

42:14.020 --> 42:16.160
Okay, so how do researchers actually do that?

42:16.360 --> 42:25.740
Well, the paper goes further by outlining three distinct mathematical formulations for how researchers can actually practically calculate this distance between futures in a neural network.

42:25.960 --> 42:29.460
Let's run through them, because I know one of them is the key to making this work.

42:29.460 --> 42:33.500
The first one they present is the baseline metric, hard Hausdorff distance.

42:33.700 --> 42:34.740
Hard Hausdorff.

42:34.860 --> 42:37.460
I'm sorry, but that sounds like an 80s action movie villain.

42:38.020 --> 42:40.240
You've messed with the wrong metric, hard Hausdorff.

42:40.520 --> 42:41.280
It really does.

42:41.380 --> 42:43.800
But mathematically, it's quite simple, though flawed.

42:44.180 --> 42:52.360
It relies on a process called beam search, which is a common algorithmic way AI explores multiple future paths simultaneously.

42:52.800 --> 42:55.340
It's a rigid binary way of looking at futures.

42:55.960 --> 42:57.820
Are the paths exactly the same?

42:57.960 --> 42:58.660
Yes or no.

42:58.660 --> 42:59.020
Okay.

42:59.020 --> 43:02.720
But the Flyxion authors quickly dismiss it as a viable metric.

43:03.360 --> 43:10.200
At the massive scales we are looking at in large language models, beam search suffers from a well-known problem called mode collapse.

43:10.360 --> 43:10.900
What's mode collapse?

43:11.200 --> 43:12.940
It gets too narrow and rigid.

43:13.340 --> 43:23.600
Two thoughts that actually share a highly similar nuanced future might seem completely disjointed mathematically just because of the rigid way the search algorithm prunes its options.

43:23.880 --> 43:26.560
It artificially inflates the distance between futures.

43:26.560 --> 43:28.300
Okay, so hard Hausdorff is out.

43:28.380 --> 43:29.040
It's too brittle.

43:29.360 --> 43:30.260
What's the next option?

43:30.260 --> 43:31.960
The second is soft Hausdorff.

43:31.960 --> 43:34.560
This is a much more practical nuanced formulation.

43:35.180 --> 43:41.140
Instead of just a hard yes or no on whether future paths match, it weights the future points by their generation probability.

43:41.140 --> 43:47.260
It acknowledges that the AI considers some futures highly likely while others are just edge cases.

43:47.260 --> 43:53.220
Soft Hausdorff measures the distance between two thoughts based on those weighted probabilistic futures.

43:53.220 --> 43:56.980
So it's much more forgiving and realistic to how probability actually works?

43:57.100 --> 43:58.320
Yes, it's a good metric.

43:59.020 --> 44:07.000
But the third formulation is the one that people ultimately champions as the theoretically superior gold standard, nucleus-Wasserstein distance.

44:07.260 --> 44:08.220
Nucleus-Wasserstein.

44:08.340 --> 44:09.740
Okay, unpack that for us.

44:10.160 --> 44:14.220
First, what is a Wasserstein distance and why the nucleus?

44:14.480 --> 44:16.840
Why is this the ultimate way to measure futures?

44:17.380 --> 44:18.660
Let's start with Wasserstein.

44:18.660 --> 44:23.020
In mathematics, the Wasserstein metric is often called the Earthmover's distance.

44:23.640 --> 44:25.620
Imagine you have two piles of dirt.

44:25.800 --> 44:26.760
Okay, two piles of dirt.

44:27.080 --> 44:34.620
The Wasserstein distance calculates the minimum amount of work required to move the dirt from pile A to perfectly recreate the shape of pile B.

44:34.960 --> 44:39.760
It is a brilliant way to measure the distance between two complex probability distributions.

44:40.020 --> 44:41.200
Wow, that makes a lot of sense.

44:41.500 --> 44:44.620
But the nucleus restriction is the true stroke of genius here.

44:44.680 --> 44:45.200
The nucleus.

44:45.200 --> 44:52.740
When an AI is generating a future, it assigns a probability to literally every single word in its vocabulary.

44:53.860 --> 44:57.440
Most of those words, tens of thousands of them, have a near zero probability.

44:58.160 --> 45:01.520
They are technically mathematically possible, but logically absurd.

45:01.660 --> 45:03.160
Like the refrigerator example earlier.

45:03.240 --> 45:03.760
Exactly.

45:04.420 --> 45:09.120
The nucleus-Wasserstein metric says let's stop wasting computing power on the absurdities.

45:09.120 --> 45:17.340
Let's only look at the nucleus of probability, say, the minimal set of highly probable words that cover 95% of the likelihood.

45:17.700 --> 45:22.480
So we intentionally ignore the 5% of crazy, hallucinated edge cases.

45:22.740 --> 45:25.000
We chop off the long tail of bad ideas.

45:25.100 --> 45:25.540
Exactly.

45:26.040 --> 45:28.660
Low probability tokens are just statistical noise.

45:28.860 --> 45:33.240
They do not represent genuinely, practically reachable futures in the admissibility field.

45:33.320 --> 45:33.900
That's brilliant.

45:33.900 --> 45:43.500
By stripping away the noise, focusing only on the dense 95% nucleus, and then using the optimal transport math of the Wasserstein metric to compare the nuclei of two different states,

45:43.720 --> 45:48.700
you get an incredibly clean, true measure of whether two thoughts are heading to the same destination.

45:48.960 --> 45:49.840
That is brilliant.

45:50.240 --> 45:57.660
So this entirely new mathematical framework, the admissibility field, the physics of renormalization and critical scales,

45:57.660 --> 46:03.280
the nucleus-wasserstein distance, it essentially provides a unified theory.

46:03.500 --> 46:04.120
It really does.

46:04.260 --> 46:07.940
It explains the weird behavior of large language models flawlessly.

46:08.480 --> 46:14.600
It explains why they forget details, how they aggressively compress data based on variants,

46:14.700 --> 46:18.780
and how their thoughts are actually trajectories into the future rather than dots on a map.

46:18.920 --> 46:19.420
Exactly.

46:19.880 --> 46:22.920
But here's the ultimate twist of this entire paper.

46:22.920 --> 46:31.100
And this is where this deep dive goes from an interesting computer science discussion to something incredibly profound about the nature of intelligence itself.

46:31.300 --> 46:31.640
Yes.

46:31.940 --> 46:39.400
Because this paper claims that this phenomenon, this dynamic traversal, this specific mathematical signature of compression, isn't just about artificial intelligence.

46:39.460 --> 46:39.780
No.

46:40.140 --> 46:41.880
The Flyxion authors make a bold leap.

46:41.980 --> 46:45.300
They claim this might be a universal law of limited capacity systems.

46:45.660 --> 46:45.900
Period.

46:46.440 --> 46:48.180
And they don't just guess at this.

46:48.300 --> 46:50.540
They pull in completely independent evidence.

46:50.540 --> 46:57.060
The Flyxion paper cites an entirely independent study from a completely different field of science to back up their math.

46:57.600 --> 47:04.000
They cite a study by Griffin et al. published in the journal Nature Mental Health earlier in 2026.

47:04.500 --> 47:04.800
Yes.

47:05.220 --> 47:10.380
The Griffin-El study was looking at a phenomenon called cortical renormalization in autism.

47:10.880 --> 47:19.160
And when you lay the biological findings of Griffin next to the computational math of Flyxion, the parallels are absolutely stunning.

47:19.160 --> 47:23.380
I want to spend some real time here because this is where the hair on the back of my neck stood up.

47:23.640 --> 47:25.080
Set the stage for the Griffin study.

47:25.220 --> 47:26.660
Let's leave AI behind for a minute.

47:26.940 --> 47:28.860
What exactly were these neuroscientists doing?

47:29.200 --> 47:32.740
Griffin and his colleagues were conducting a massive pediatric neuroscience study.

47:33.200 --> 47:40.300
They ran high-density EEG decoding on a cohort of 399 children, ranging in age from 6 to 11 years old.

47:40.300 --> 47:50.800
For those who might not know, an EEG, an electroencephalogram, involves placing a net of electrodes on the scalp to read the electrical activity, the brainwaves happening inside the cortex, right?

47:51.080 --> 47:51.380
Yes.

47:51.720 --> 47:57.720
And high-density arrays allow for very precise special and temporal mapping of that electrical activity.

47:58.280 --> 48:03.160
The study cohort included both neurotypical children and autistic children.

48:03.340 --> 48:03.700
Okay.

48:03.700 --> 48:07.760
The experimental task was simple, a classic neuroscience setup.

48:08.240 --> 48:10.800
They showed the children a rapid series of images.

48:11.400 --> 48:13.960
Some images were faces and some images were houses.

48:14.260 --> 48:15.360
Faces and houses.

48:15.620 --> 48:19.660
While the children watched the screen, the researchers recorded their brainwaves,

48:19.820 --> 48:27.440
and then they used complex machine learning classifiers to decode those brainwaves to see how the children's brains were internally representing the images.

48:27.440 --> 48:36.800
So instead of using artificial probes to look at hidden states in an AI, they are using physical sensors to look at biological human brainwaves.

48:36.960 --> 48:40.160
They are trying to read the brain's internal representations.

48:40.400 --> 48:40.860
Exactly.

48:41.040 --> 48:42.660
What was the shocking parallel they found?

48:42.800 --> 48:44.980
What connected the carbon to the silicon?

48:45.180 --> 48:50.980
They found the exact same two-level hierarchy of collapse that Sedeck and Fard found in the AI language models.

48:51.100 --> 48:51.580
You're kidding.

48:51.700 --> 48:53.620
It's the exact same mathematical pattern.

48:53.920 --> 48:54.780
It is identical.

48:54.780 --> 49:06.480
Across all ages in the study and across all time windows of the brainwave response, the autistic children showed beautifully preserved, highly robust, coarse discrimination.

49:07.320 --> 49:13.040
Their brainwaves clearly and instantly distinguished between the category of face and the category of house.

49:13.400 --> 49:15.520
That is the task-level identity.

49:15.720 --> 49:16.860
It was perfectly preserved.

49:16.860 --> 49:27.920
But when the researchers zoomed in and looked for fine discrimination, when they tried to see if the brainwaves could distinguish between face A and face B or house A and house B, that fine discrimination was completely absent.

49:28.440 --> 49:31.200
The instance-level identity was entirely collapsed.

49:31.320 --> 49:31.720
Hold on.

49:31.780 --> 49:33.100
I want to make sure the weight of this lands.

49:33.700 --> 49:35.440
Here's where it gets really interesting.

49:35.620 --> 49:43.740
The artificial intelligence perfectly preserved the overarching task of translation, but completely merged and forgot the specific sentences it was translating.

49:43.740 --> 49:54.520
The autistic children's biological brains perfectly preserved the overarching category of face, but mathematically merged the specific individual faces they were looking at.

49:54.660 --> 49:55.080
Yes.

49:55.200 --> 49:58.380
It is the exact same signature of distinguishability collapse.

49:58.900 --> 50:00.140
The task survives.

50:00.420 --> 50:01.460
The instance vanishes.

50:01.840 --> 50:02.260
Exactly.

50:02.260 --> 50:09.580
The mathematics of what is happening inside the network, whether it is made of artificial neurons or biological neurons, appears to be the same.

50:09.580 --> 50:19.080
And the developmental trajectory of the neurotypical children in the study provides incredible proof for the concept of the renormalization curve we discussed earlier.

50:19.580 --> 50:19.600
Right.

50:19.660 --> 50:24.220
Because the neurotypical kids in the study didn't show this collapse across the board, did they?

50:24.320 --> 50:29.000
They eventually did develop that fine-grained discrimination between face A and face B, right?

50:29.100 --> 50:29.320
Yes.

50:29.380 --> 50:30.660
But the timing is the key.

50:30.660 --> 50:43.280
The neurotypical children showed both levels of discrimination, but the fine-grained instance-level discrimination, face A versus face B, only fully emerged in the older cohort of children around age 10 to 11.

50:43.440 --> 50:44.980
It took years to develop.

50:45.400 --> 50:52.020
So how does the Flyxion paper explain this biological development through the lens of their mathematical AI conjecture?

50:52.320 --> 50:54.680
How does a brain learn to see the details?

50:55.120 --> 50:58.780
They explain it by equating life experience to AI training data.

50:58.980 --> 50:59.400
Oh, wow.

50:59.400 --> 51:03.240
In biological systems, experience is the optimization process.

51:03.560 --> 51:06.180
It's the equivalent of the AI reading billions of words.

51:06.520 --> 51:15.700
For neurotypical children, consistent, highly repetitive social exposure to faces over years and years of development acts as a massive, consistent training signal.

51:16.180 --> 51:20.660
The brain is constantly rewarded for recognizing mom versus dad, friend versus stranger.

51:20.660 --> 51:20.980
Right.

51:20.980 --> 51:27.940
This intense, consistent signal eventually drives their critical scale, R epsilon sub C, from the physics model downward.

51:28.580 --> 51:39.660
As the scale drops lower and lower over the years, their neural capacity is forced to process finer and finer distinctions, eventually separating face A from face B permanently in the neural architecture.

51:39.660 --> 51:51.700
The optimization pressure of human social life literally forces their biological hard drive to allocate precious memory to the fine details of human faces, because society relentlessly rewards that specific skill.

51:51.840 --> 51:52.340
Precisely.

51:52.340 --> 51:53.800
But what about the autistic children?

51:54.120 --> 51:58.120
How does the mathematical model explain their sustained collapse of the instance level details?

51:58.120 --> 52:02.360
The conjecture suggests that in autistic children, the training signal is different.

52:02.360 --> 52:17.460
Reduced social attention, perhaps looking at faces less frequently, or looking at different parts of the environment instead of social cues, means the developing brain receives a significantly less consistent, much higher variance training signal for fine-grained facial identity.

52:17.600 --> 52:17.780
Okay.

52:18.140 --> 52:25.260
Because the signal is noisy or less frequent, the optimization process of the brain performs the exact same calculation the AI did.

52:25.260 --> 52:36.340
It looks at its limited capacity and essentially decides that memorizing the exact geometry of individual faces isn't worth the energy because it isn't being rewarded as consistently.

52:36.680 --> 52:39.840
So their critical scale, Epsilon sub C, remains elevated.

52:40.260 --> 52:44.860
The fine details of specific faces remain below the critical scale, and they are integrated out.

52:45.280 --> 52:51.580
The brain smooths over them to save space for other things, just like the AI integrated out the specific sentence it was translating.

52:51.860 --> 52:53.740
That is exactly how the math maps out.

52:53.740 --> 52:56.800
That is a staggering way to view neurodevelopment.

52:56.880 --> 53:05.140
And there is even more physical undeniable proof of this trajectory theory hidden in the actual grid of the EEG data itself isn't there.

53:05.280 --> 53:08.720
The paper mentions something called temporal generalization matrices.

53:09.080 --> 53:09.340
Yes.

53:09.680 --> 53:13.400
This is perhaps the most definitive piece of evidence linking the two domains.

53:13.580 --> 53:13.700
Wow.

53:13.780 --> 53:19.560
A temporal generalization matrix is a specific way neuroscientists plot EEG data.

53:20.260 --> 53:21.080
Imagine a grid.

53:21.080 --> 53:26.620
The y-axis is the exact millisecond the machine learning classifier was trained on the brainwave.

53:26.940 --> 53:30.040
The x-axis is the exact millisecond it is tested on.

53:30.780 --> 53:41.180
If a neural representation is a static state, an x-ray that just turns on and stays on the classifier trained at 100 milliseconds will still work perfectly at 200, 300, and 400 milliseconds.

53:41.540 --> 53:44.660
The data on the grid forms a solid, stable square shape.

53:44.800 --> 53:46.300
Because the picture hasn't changed.

53:46.300 --> 53:46.820
Exactly.

53:47.340 --> 53:57.300
But when Griffin's team looked at the EEG data for the neurotypical children successfully processing the fine details of faces they didn't see a square, they found a strict diagonal decoding pattern.

53:57.600 --> 54:01.580
The classifier trained at 100 milliseconds only worked at 100 milliseconds.

54:01.920 --> 54:03.860
It failed at 120 milliseconds.

54:04.160 --> 54:04.600
Oh, wow.

54:04.600 --> 54:13.640
In neuroscience, a strong diagonal pattern over time is absolute proof that the neural representation is sequentially evolving.

54:14.260 --> 54:17.500
It is dynamically changing millisecond by millisecond.

54:17.680 --> 54:18.360
It's a trajectory.

54:18.700 --> 54:23.220
